.TH "LIBPKGSTATE\-APPLY" "3" "" "libpkgstate\-apply" "Version 3.1.0"
.SH NAME
libpkgstate\-apply \- admit completed native application as installed state
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]#include <libpkgstate\-apply/state_projection.h>\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]#include <libpkgstate\-apply/adapter.h>\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]project_completed_application()\f[R] validates one completed native \f[B]libpkgapply\f[R] operation against the exact operation\-specific request, accepted plan, target context, lease\-bound state projection, expected native snapshot, ownership universe, and completed path consequences.
It returns an immutable \f[B]state_publication_request\f[R] and performs no store I/O.
.PP
Additive overloads accept one exact \f[B]transaction_evidence_identity\f[R].
When supplied, transaction evidence is retained by the publication request and, for installation or upgrade, by the durable installation receipt.
The same evidence is supplied to both objects during construction; it is never attached after projection.
.SH LEASE\-BOUND STATE READ
\f[B]read_application_state()\f[R] accepts one exact \f[B]package_application_request\f[R], one caller\-owned \f[B]target_mutation_lease\f[R], and one \f[B]canonical_store\f[R].
It first proves that the lease is live and belongs to the request target and exclusion domain, then performs exactly one canonical store read.
The returned \f[B]lease_bound_application_state\f[R] owns both the storage\-derived \f[B]snapshot\f[R] and the matching \f[B]lease_bound_state_projection\f[R] so they cannot be paired with different state epochs.
.PP
The projection contains the accepted plan\[aq]s exact path universe and the current installed owners derived from the canonical snapshot.
It is complete only when the snapshot identity, ownership inventory, target binding, and every path\-owner set agree with the accepted plan.
Projection evidence is derived canonically from the request, target, lease acquisition, target binding, state epoch, ownership inventory, and path\-owner closure.
A caller does not supply that evidence identity.
.PP
The lease is checked before and after the state read and again before return.
A lost or foreign lease is a typed projection refusal.
Store read failures retain their native \f[B]store_error\f[R] classification.
The function does not acquire a lease, initialize a store, inspect the target filesystem, perform application, publish state, reconcile, or repair.
.PP
Restart\-time historical projection is not reconstructed by this boundary.
The durable \f[B]libpkgapply\f[R] application journal owns the exact projection admitted before mutation.
A caller may use \f[B]read_application_state()\f[R] under a newly held lease only to observe current canonical pre\-state; that current observation is never rebased onto an old lease identity.
.SH INCOMING AUTHORITY
Installation and upgrade requests retain an \f[B]incoming_package_authority\f[R] that contains one complete \f[B]libpkgbuild\-plan\f[R] projection.
That projection retains the exact \f[B]libpkgbuild\-image\f[R] admission together with source\-derived candidate and artifact facts established before mutation.
.PP
The adapter passes the retained build\-image authority directly through \f[B]libpkgstate\-build\f[R].
That boundary derives the state source record and build provenance from the exact sealed source, resolver\-backed request, successful build result, and independently inspected image.
It accepts no separate caller\-supplied source, build result, image, or provenance value and never reconstructs build provenance from planner facts.
.PP
Installation additionally takes one typed initial installation reason.
Upgrade preserves the prior installed reason.
Removal accepts no incoming package or installation reason.
.SH OWNERSHIP
Complete object metadata comes from completed application evidence.
Active origin comes from the accepted active outcome.
Rejected\-object identity and side come from durable completed rejected\-object evidence.
Publication fails when an owned path lacks complete publication\-eligible object truth or when application evidence grants unplanned ownership.
.SH IDENTITY
Planner and application identities are translated into typed state references.
State\-owned installed control, receipt, package, snapshot, request, and publication identities are computed by \f[B]libpkgstate\f[R].
.PP
The overloads without transaction evidence remain authoritative for operations outside an effectful transaction session and preserve their existing identity semantics.
